WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.800
Machine learning is a method of data analysis&nbsp;
that automates analytical model building.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.640
It is a branch of artificial intelligence based&nbsp;
on the idea that systems can learn from data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.320
identify patterns and make decisions&nbsp;
with minimal human intervention.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.720
Because of new computing technologies, machine&nbsp;
learning today is not like machine learning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:32.240
the past. It was born from pattern recognition&nbsp;
and the theory that computers can learn without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.920
being programmed to perform specific tasks.&nbsp;
The iterative aspect of machine learning is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.240
important because as models are exposed to&nbsp;
new data, they are able to independently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:48.320
adapt. They learn from previous computations&nbsp;
to produce reliable, repeatable decisions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.280
results. It’s a science that’s not new,&nbsp;
but one that has gained fresh momentum.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.400
While many machine learning algorithms&nbsp;
have been around for a long time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:03.120
the ability to automatically apply complex&nbsp;
mathematical calculations to big data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:09.040
over and over, faster and faster, is a recent&nbsp;
development. Here are a few widely publicized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:15.760
examples of machine learning applications you may&nbsp;
be familiar with. For example, the heavily hyped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:21.920
self-driving Google car, online recommendation&nbsp;
offers such as those from Amazon and Netflix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.560
knowing what customers are saying about you on&nbsp;
Twitter, and fraud detection, One of the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:34.480
obvious, important uses in our world today.
Why is machine learning important?&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.960
Resurging interest in machine learning is due to&nbsp;
the same factors that have made data mining and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.960
Bayesian analysis more popular than ever. Things&nbsp;
like growing volumes and varieties of available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.160
data, computational processing that is cheaper&nbsp;
and more powerful, and affordable data storage.&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.440
&nbsp;
All of these things mean it's possible to quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.800
and automatically produce models that can analyze&nbsp;
bigger, more complex data and deliver faster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:07.440
more accurate results, even on a very large scale.&nbsp;
And by building precise models, an organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.320
has a better chance of identifying profitable&nbsp;
opportunities, or avoiding unknown risks.&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.040
To create good machine learning systems, you&nbsp;
will need Data preparation capabilities ,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:26.640
basic and advanced Algorithms, Automation and&nbsp;
iterative processes,Scalability,And, modeling.&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:32.960
There are some differences in nomenclature used&nbsp;
in machine learning versus traditional statistics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:40.560
For example, In machine learning, a target is&nbsp;
called a label. In statistics, a target is called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.840
a dependent variable. A variable in statistics&nbsp;
is called a feature in machine learning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.640
And, A transformation in statistics is&nbsp;
called feature creation in machine learning.&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.080
Two of the most widely adopted machine&nbsp;
learning methods are supervised learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.080
and unsupervised learning, but there are&nbsp;
also other methods of machine learning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.840
Here's an overview of the most popular types.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:09.920
Supervised learning algorithms are trained using&nbsp;
labeled examples, such as an input where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:16.000
desired output is known. For example, a piece of&nbsp;
equipment could have data points labeled either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:22.160
failed or runs. The learning algorithm receives a&nbsp;
set of inputs along with the corresponding correct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:27.760
outputs, and the algorithm learns by comparing its&nbsp;
actual output with correct outputs to find errors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:34.400
It then modifies the model accordingly. Through&nbsp;
methods like classification, regression,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:39.360
prediction and gradient boosting, supervised&nbsp;
learning uses patterns to predict the values of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:45.280
the label on additional unlabeled data. Supervised&nbsp;
learning is commonly used in applications where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:51.680
historical data predicts likely future events.&nbsp;
For example, it can anticipate when credit card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.560
transactions are likely to be fraudulent or which&nbsp;
insurance customer is likely to file a claim.&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:02.240
Unsupervised learning is used against&nbsp;
data that has no historical labels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.640
The system is not told the "right answer." The&nbsp;
algorithm must figure out what is being shown.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.720
The goal is to explore the data&nbsp;
and find some structure within.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:20.160
Unsupervised learning works well on transactional&nbsp;
data. For example, it can identify segments of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.640
customers with similar attributes who can then&nbsp;
be treated similarly in marketing campaigns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.360
Or it can find the main attributes that&nbsp;
separate customer segments from each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.120
Popular techniques include self-organizing&nbsp;
maps, nearest-neighbor mapping, k-means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:41.840
clustering and singular value decomposition. These&nbsp;
algorithms are also used to segment text topics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:49.120
recommend items and identify data outliers.
Reinforcement learning is often used for robotics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:54.480
gaming and navigation. With reinforcement&nbsp;
learning, the algorithm discovers through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.360
trial and error which actions yield the&nbsp;
greatest rewards. This type of learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:05.440
has three primary components: the agent, the&nbsp;
learner or decision maker. the environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:12.080
everything the agent interacts with. and actions,&nbsp;
what the agent can do). The objective is for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:16.480
agent to choose actions that maximize the&nbsp;
expected reward over a given amount of time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:20.960
The agent will reach the goal much&nbsp;
faster by following a good policy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:32.560
So the goal in reinforcement&nbsp;
learning is to learn the best policy.

